/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ckad.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ckad", [["ck1",0,1,"A developer needs to run a database migration once and have Kubernetes retry it if the container fails, then stop. Which resource fits best?",["Deployment","DaemonSet","Job","ReplicaSet"],2,"A Job runs Pods to successful completion and retries failures up to backoffLimit. A Deployment would restart the finished container forever to keep replicas running.","Workload resources",["A Deployment keeps a set number of replicas running forever, so a finished migration container would just be restarted again and again.","A DaemonSet runs one long-lived Pod per node; it has no notion of running a task once to completion.",null,"A ReplicaSet maintains a fixed count of running Pods and restarts them when they exit, so it never treats a run as finished."]],["ck2",0,1,"A team must run a log-collection agent on every node, including nodes added later. Which workload resource should they use?",["DaemonSet","CronJob","StatefulSet","Deployment"],0,"A DaemonSet schedules one Pod per eligible node and adds Pods automatically when nodes join. A Deployment spreads replicas without guaranteeing one per node.","Workload resources",[null,"A CronJob runs Jobs on a schedule that terminate; it does not keep an agent running on every node.","A StatefulSet gives replicas stable names and storage but places them wherever the scheduler chooses, not one per node.","A Deployment spreads a fixed replica count across nodes with no guarantee of one per node, and it does not grow when nodes are added."]],["ck3",0,1,"A clustered database needs stable Pod names (db-0, db-1) and its own PersistentVolumeClaim per replica. Which resource provides both?",["Job with parallelism set to 3","Deployment with one shared ReadWriteMany PVC","DaemonSet with a hostPath volume","StatefulSet with volumeClaimTemplates"],3,"StatefulSets give ordinal, stable identities and create a PVC per replica from volumeClaimTemplates. A Deployment's replicas are interchangeable and would share one claim.","Workload resources",["A Job runs Pods to completion; parallelism only controls concurrency and gives neither stable names nor per-replica claims.","Deployment Pods get random name suffixes, and a single shared RWX claim is the opposite of one PVC per replica.","A DaemonSet runs one Pod per node, and hostPath ties data to a node's filesystem rather than giving each replica its own claim.",null]],["ck4",0,1,"A report CronJob must run every day at 02:30. Which schedule is correct?",["2 30 * * *","30 2 * * *","* * 2 30 *","0 2,30 * * *"],1,"Cron fields are minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week, so 30 2 * * * is 02:30 daily. \"2 30 * * *\" puts 30 in the hour field, which is invalid.","CronJobs",["This puts 2 in the minute field and 30 in the hour field, which is invalid because hours only go from 0 to 23.",null,"This sets minute and hour to every value and puts 2 and 30 in the day-of-month and month fields, which is wrong and 30 is an invalid month.","This runs at minute 0 of hours 2 and 30; hour 30 is invalid, and even 02:00 is not 02:30."]],["ck5",0,1,"A CronJob sometimes runs longer than its interval, and overlapping runs corrupt data. Which setting prevents a new run from starting while the previous one is still active?",["concurrencyPolicy: Replace","successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 0","startingDeadlineSeconds: 0","concurrencyPolicy: Forbid"],3,"Forbid skips the new run if the previous Job is still running. Replace would kill the running Job and start a new one, which is still an interrupted run.","CronJobs",["Replace cancels the running Job and starts the new one, so runs still interrupt each other instead of being prevented from starting.","This only controls how many finished successful Jobs are kept; it has no effect on whether runs overlap.","startingDeadlineSeconds limits how late a missed run may start; it does not check whether a previous Job is still active.",null]],["ck6",0,1,"You want to test a CronJob named nightly right now without waiting for its schedule. Which command does this?",["kubectl run nightly --restart=OnFailure","kubectl rollout restart cronjob/nightly","kubectl create job test-run --from=cronjob/nightly","kubectl patch cronjob nightly -p '{\"spec\":{\"suspend\":false}}'"],2,"`kubectl create job <name> --from=cronjob/<cj>` creates a Job from the CronJob's jobTemplate immediately. Unsuspending only lets future scheduled runs happen.","CronJobs",["kubectl run creates a standalone Pod from an image and knows nothing about the CronJob's jobTemplate.","rollout restart works on Deployments, StatefulSets and DaemonSets; CronJobs have no rollouts to restart.",null,"Setting suspend to false only re-enables future scheduled runs; nothing runs until the next schedule time."]],["ck7",0,1,"A Job spec has `completions: 6` and `parallelism: 2`. How does it run?",["Six Pods start at once and two must succeed","Up to two Pods run at a time until six have succeeded","Two Pods run in total, each retried six times","Six Pods run one after another, each one repeated twice"],1,"completions is the number of successful Pods required and parallelism caps how many run concurrently. It is not a retry count; retries are controlled by backoffLimit.","Jobs",["Parallelism 2 caps concurrent Pods, so six cannot start at once, and completions 6 means six successes are required, not two.",null,"Neither field is a retry count; retries are controlled by backoffLimit, and six successful Pods are required.","Parallelism 2 allows two Pods at a time rather than strictly one after another, and nothing repeats each Pod twice."]],["ck8",0,1,"A Job Pod template sets `restartPolicy: Always` and the API server rejects it. Why?",["Jobs require restartPolicy Never or OnFailure","Jobs require the restartPolicy field to be omitted","Always is only valid for init containers in Jobs","restartPolicy must be set on each container, not the Pod"],0,"Job Pods must terminate, so only Never or OnFailure are allowed. Always is the default for Deployments, which keep containers running.","Jobs",[null,"Omitting restartPolicy defaults it to Always, which Jobs reject, so a Job template must set Never or OnFailure explicitly.","restartPolicy: Always on an init container defines a native sidecar, which does not change the Pod-level rule that Job Pods cannot use Always.","restartPolicy is a Pod-level field; the per-container field only exists for init containers acting as sidecars, and it is not the reason for the rejection."]],["ck9",0,1,"An image has `ENTRYPOINT [\"python\"]` and `CMD [\"app.py\"]`. The Pod sets `args: [\"worker.py\"]` and no command. What runs?",["python worker.py","worker.py with no interpreter","python app.py worker.py","python app.py"],0,"args replaces CMD while the image ENTRYPOINT is kept, giving python worker.py. Setting command instead would have replaced ENTRYPOINT.","Container images",[null,"args does not touch the ENTRYPOINT, so python is still the executable and worker.py is passed to it.","args replaces CMD rather than appending to it, so app.py is dropped.","This is what runs with no overrides; setting args replaces the CMD app.py with worker.py."]],["ck10",0,1,"A Go service's image is 900 MB because it contains the compiler and source code. What is the best way to shrink it?",["Add a .dockerignore listing the go.mod file","Squash layers by chaining every RUN instruction with &&","Run the container with a lower memory limit","Use a multi-stage build that copies only the binary"],3,"A multi-stage build compiles in one stage and copies just the binary into a small runtime image. Chaining RUN commands trims a few layers but still ships the whole toolchain.","Container images",["go.mod is tiny and needed for the build; ignoring it breaks the build and does nothing about the toolchain in the image.","Chaining RUN commands reduces layer count a little, but the compiler and source are still shipped in the final image.","A memory limit controls runtime resource use and has no effect on how large the image is on disk.",null]],["ck11",0,1,"On the exam you must build an image from ./app tagged myapp:2.0 and save it as /tmp/myapp.tar. Which docker (or podman) commands do this?",["docker create myapp:2.0 ./app, then docker export myapp:2.0 > /tmp/myapp.tar","docker build -t myapp:2.0 ./app, then docker save -o /tmp/myapp.tar myapp:2.0","docker build ./app --name myapp:2.0, then docker push /tmp/myapp.tar","docker commit ./app myapp:2.0, then docker load -i /tmp/myapp.tar"],1,"build -t tags the image and save -o writes the image (all layers) to a tarball. export works on containers, not images, and load does the opposite of save.","Container images",["docker create makes a container, not an image from a build context, and docker export flattens a container's filesystem rather than saving a tagged image.",null,"docker build has no --name flag for tagging (that is -t), and docker push uploads to a registry rather than writing a tar file.","docker commit snapshots a running container, not a directory, and docker load reads a tar into the image store instead of writing one."]],["ck12",0,1,"A Pod must wait until a database Service resolves in DNS before the app container starts. Which design fits?",["A sidecar container that loops forever","A postStart lifecycle hook on the application container","An init container that loops until nslookup succeeds","A readiness probe on the database Service"],2,"Init containers run to completion before app containers start, so a DNS check loop there blocks startup. postStart runs alongside the entrypoint and does not delay it.","Multi-container Pods",["A sidecar runs alongside the app container, so a loop there does not delay the app from starting.","postStart runs concurrently with the container's entrypoint, so the app can start before DNS is ready.",null,"Services do not have probes; probes belong to containers, and a readiness probe never blocks another container from starting."]],["ck13",0,1,"In current Kubernetes, how do you declare a native sidecar that starts before the app and keeps running for the Pod's lifetime?",["Add it under containers with the label role: sidecar","Add it under initContainers with restartPolicy: Always","Add it under ephemeralContainers in the Pod spec","Set shareProcessNamespace: true on the Pod"],1,"A native sidecar is an init container with its own restartPolicy: Always, so it starts first and is not waited on to finish. Labels have no special meaning to the kubelet.","Multi-container Pods",["The kubelet ignores labels when deciding container behavior, so this is just an ordinary app container with no startup ordering.",null,"Ephemeral containers are temporary debugging containers added to a running Pod with kubectl debug, not part of normal startup.","shareProcessNamespace lets containers see each other's processes; it does not make a container start first or act as a sidecar."]],["ck14",0,1,"Two containers in one Pod must share scratch files, and the data can be lost when the Pod is deleted. Which volume type is simplest?",["hostPath","persistentVolumeClaim","configMap","emptyDir"],3,"emptyDir is created with the Pod, shared by its containers and deleted with it. hostPath would tie the Pod to one node's filesystem for no benefit.","Volumes",["hostPath mounts a directory from the node, which ties the Pod to that node and leaves data behind after the Pod is gone.","A PVC gives durable storage that outlives the Pod, which is more setup than needed for throwaway scratch files.","A configMap volume is populated from a ConfigMap and is read-only, so containers cannot use it to exchange scratch files.",null]],["ck15",0,1,"Read this snippet: `volumes: [{name: cache, emptyDir: {medium: Memory, sizeLimit: 64Mi}}]`. What is true about the volume?",["It is backed by tmpfs and counts toward memory use","It is stored on the node's disk and survives restarts","It is a PVC that requests 64Mi from the StorageClass","It is shared across every Pod on the node"],0,"medium: Memory mounts the emptyDir as tmpfs, so its contents use RAM and count against the container's memory. It is still per-Pod and ephemeral.","Volumes",[null,"medium: Memory means tmpfs in RAM, not node disk, and emptyDir contents are deleted when the Pod is removed.","emptyDir is not a claim and does not involve a StorageClass; sizeLimit just caps how much the volume may hold.","An emptyDir belongs to one Pod and is shared only by that Pod's containers, not by other Pods on the node."]],["ck16",0,1,"A PVC with `accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]` is bound. What does ReadWriteOnce guarantee?",["Only one Pod in the cluster may ever read it","It can be written once and then becomes read-only","It can be mounted read-write by a single node","Every node can mount it read-write at once"],2,"ReadWriteOnce means read-write by a single node; several Pods on that node can still use it. ReadWriteOncePod is the mode that limits it to one Pod.","Volumes",["RWO restricts mounting to one node, not one Pod, and says nothing about the whole cluster; ReadWriteOncePod is the single-Pod mode.","Access modes describe how many nodes can mount a volume; none of them makes data write-once then read-only.",null,"That describes ReadWriteMany; ReadWriteOnce limits read-write mounting to a single node."]],["ck17",0,1,"A Pod stays Pending and describe shows \"pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims\". The PVC names storageClassName: fast, which does not exist. What fixes it?",["Delete and recreate the Pod","Add a readiness probe to the Pod","Change the PVC to use an existing StorageClass","Add a nodeSelector so the Pod lands on a storage node"],2,"Without a matching StorageClass nothing provisions a volume, so the claim stays Pending. storageClassName on a PVC cannot be edited in place, so recreate the PVC with a valid class; recreating the Pod alone changes nothing.","Volumes",["The new Pod would reference the same unbound claim and stay Pending, because no StorageClass exists to provision a volume.","Readiness probes only matter after a container runs; they cannot help a Pod that is not scheduled because of an unbound claim.",null,"The problem is that no volume is provisioned for the claim, not node placement; a nodeSelector cannot create the missing StorageClass."]],["ck18",0,1,"In a Pod spec, a volumeMount uses `name: data` but the volumes list only contains `name: app-data`. What happens?",["The API rejects the Pod as invalid","The mount silently uses an emptyDir","The kubelet mounts app-data because it is the only volume","The Pod starts and the mount path is left empty"],0,"Every volumeMount name must match a volume defined in the Pod, so validation fails when the Pod is created. Nothing is guessed or defaulted.","Volumes",[null,"Kubernetes never creates a default volume for an unmatched mount name; the Pod is rejected during validation.","The kubelet does not guess which volume you meant; names must match exactly or the API server rejects the spec.","The Pod never gets created, because the API server validates volumeMount names before the Pod is stored."]],["ck19",0,2,"Deployment web runs container web with nginx:1.25. Which command starts a rolling update to nginx:1.27?",["kubectl replace deployment web nginx:1.27","kubectl rollout update deployment/web --image=nginx:1.27","kubectl edit pod web --image=nginx:1.27","kubectl set image deployment/web web=nginx:1.27"],3,"`kubectl set image deployment/<name> <container>=<image>` changes the Pod template, which triggers a rollout. Editing a Pod does not change the Deployment and is overwritten.","Rolling updates",["kubectl replace needs a full manifest file with -f; it does not take a Deployment name and image as arguments.","There is no rollout update subcommand, and --image is not a rollout flag.","kubectl edit has no --image flag, and changing a Pod does not change the Deployment's template, so the ReplicaSet would replace it.",null]],["ck20",0,2,"After a bad release, you need Deployment api back on revision 3 specifically. Which command?",["kubectl rollout history deployment/api --revision=3","kubectl rollout undo deployment/api --to-revision=3","kubectl rollout restart deployment/api --revision=3","kubectl apply deployment/api --revision=3"],1,"rollout undo with --to-revision rolls the template back to that revision. history with --revision only shows details of it.","Rolling updates",["rollout history with --revision only prints the details of that revision; it does not roll anything back.",null,"rollout restart re-creates Pods with the current template and has no --revision flag.","kubectl apply needs a manifest file and has no --revision option for returning to an earlier Deployment revision."]],["ck21",0,2,"A Deployment has 4 replicas, `maxSurge: 1` and `maxUnavailable: 0`. What is the maximum number of Pods during a rollout?",["3","4","8","5"],3,"maxSurge allows one Pod above the desired 4, and maxUnavailable 0 means none are removed before replacements are ready, so the peak is 5.","Rolling updates",["maxUnavailable 0 means the count never drops below the desired 4, so 3 is impossible during the rollout.","4 is the desired count; maxSurge 1 lets the controller create one extra Pod above it.","maxSurge 1 allows only one extra Pod, not a full second set of 4.",null]],["ck22",0,2,"An app cannot run two versions at the same time because of a schema lock. Downtime is acceptable. Which Deployment strategy fits?",["RollingUpdate with maxSurge 25%","RollingUpdate with maxUnavailable 0","Recreate","A canary with one replica"],2,"Recreate terminates all old Pods before creating new ones, so versions never overlap. Any RollingUpdate setting runs old and new Pods together for a while.","Deployment strategies",["A rolling update with surge always runs old and new Pods side by side, which the schema lock forbids.","maxUnavailable 0 keeps old Pods running until new ones are ready, so versions overlap for the whole rollout.",null,"A canary deliberately runs the new version alongside the old one, which is exactly what the lock does not allow."]],["ck23",0,2,"You run web-blue and web-green Deployments, labeled version=blue and version=green. How do you cut all traffic over to green at once?",["Scale web-blue to zero, then wait for rollout","Point the Service selector at version=green","Run kubectl rollout undo on web-blue","Add version=green to the web-blue Pod template"],1,"In blue/green the Service selector decides which set receives traffic, and patching it switches everything in one step while blue stays ready for rollback. Scaling blue down first causes an outage window.","Deployment strategies",["Scaling blue to zero before traffic is switched creates an outage window and is not the one-step cutover blue/green relies on.",null,"rollout undo changes web-blue's template back to an older revision; it does not move traffic to web-green.","Relabeling blue's Pods as green would make the old version receive traffic meant for green, which defeats the switch."]],["ck24",0,2,"Service shop selects app=shop. Deployment shop-v1 has 9 replicas and shop-v2 has 1, both labeled app=shop. What is this setup?",["A canary sending about 10% of traffic to v2","A blue/green deployment","A Recreate rollout in progress","A StatefulSet partition update"],0,"Both versions match the Service selector, so traffic splits roughly by Pod count: 1 of 10. Blue/green would have the Service select only one version at a time.","Deployment strategies",[null,"Blue/green points the Service at one version at a time; here both versions match the selector and receive traffic together.","Recreate terminates all old Pods before starting new ones, so the two versions would not be running side by side.","These are Deployments, not a StatefulSet, and partition updates apply to StatefulSet rolling updates only."]],["ck25",0,2,"Which change to a Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet revision?",["Changing an environment variable in the Pod template","Scaling replicas from 3 to 6","Adding an annotation to the Deployment's own metadata section","Changing revisionHistoryLimit"],0,"Only changes to spec.template trigger a rollout and a new ReplicaSet. Scaling changes the replica count of the existing ReplicaSet.","Deployments",[null,"Scaling only changes the replica count of the current ReplicaSet; the Pod template is unchanged, so no new revision is created.","Annotations on the Deployment's own metadata are outside spec.template and do not trigger a rollout.","revisionHistoryLimit controls how many old ReplicaSets are kept; changing it does not alter the Pod template."]],["ck26",0,2,"You need to make several changes to a Deployment without triggering a rollout after each one. Which commands help?",["kubectl scale --replicas=0, then scale back","kubectl annotate deployment --overwrite, then kubectl rollout restart","kubectl delete deployment, then apply it again","kubectl rollout pause, apply changes, kubectl rollout resume"],3,"Pausing a rollout lets you batch template changes; resume starts one rollout with all of them. Scaling to zero causes downtime.","Deployments",["Scaling to zero causes downtime and does not stop each template change from triggering its own rollout.","rollout restart triggers another rollout immediately, which is the opposite of batching changes.","Deleting the Deployment takes the app down completely and throws away its revision history.",null]],["ck27",0,2,"You want to install the bitnami/nginx chart as a release named web in namespace frontend, which does not exist yet. Which command?",["helm install bitnami/nginx web -n frontend","helm install web bitnami/nginx -n frontend --create-namespace","helm create web bitnami/nginx --namespace frontend --create-namespace","helm upgrade bitnami/nginx --name web -n frontend"],1,"The syntax is helm install <release> <chart>, and --create-namespace makes the namespace. helm create scaffolds a new local chart instead.","Helm",["The argument order is wrong: helm install takes the release name first and the chart second, and the missing namespace is not created.",null,"helm create scaffolds a new local chart directory; it does not install a chart from a repository.","helm upgrade takes <release> <chart> and has no --name flag in Helm 3; it also needs an existing release unless --install is used."]],["ck28",0,2,"Before installing a chart, you want to see which settings you can override. Which command lists its default values?",["helm get values <chart>","helm list --values","helm show values <chart>","helm status <chart>"],2,"helm show values prints the chart's values.yaml. helm get values reads the values of an installed release, not a chart.","Helm",["helm get values reads the user-supplied values of an installed release, not the defaults of a chart.","helm list shows installed releases and has no --values flag for listing chart settings.",null,"helm status reports the state of an installed release, not a chart's configurable values."]],["ck29",0,2,"Release api was upgraded to revision 4 and is failing. Which command returns it to revision 3?",["helm undo api --revision 3","helm rollback api 3","helm upgrade api --revision 3","helm history api 3"],1,"helm rollback <release> <revision> redeploys an earlier revision, creating a new revision 5. helm history only lists revisions.","Helm",["There is no helm undo command; rolling back is done with helm rollback.",null,"helm upgrade has no --revision flag; it installs new chart or values and always creates a new revision.","helm history lists a release's revisions but does not change anything."]],["ck30",0,2,"You must find every Helm release in the cluster, across all namespaces. Which command?",["helm search repo --all","kubectl get helm -A","helm status --all","helm list -A"],3,"helm list -A (--all-namespaces) lists releases everywhere. helm search looks for charts in repositories, not installed releases.","Helm",["helm search repo looks for charts in configured repositories, not installed releases in the cluster.","Helm releases are not a Kubernetes resource type that kubectl get can list by default.","helm status reports on one named release and has no --all flag for listing every release.",null]],["ck31",0,2,"You want to render a chart's manifests locally with your values file without installing anything. Which command?",["helm template myrel ./chart -f values.yaml","helm install --dry-run=false","helm package ./chart -f values.yaml","helm pull ./chart --render"],0,"helm template renders the templates to YAML on your machine. helm package only builds a .tgz archive of the chart.","Helm",[null,"--dry-run=false just performs a normal install, which is exactly what you wanted to avoid, and no release name or chart is given.","helm package bundles a chart directory into a .tgz archive; it does not render templates with values.","helm pull downloads a chart from a repository and has no --render option."]],["ck32",0,2,"A directory contains kustomization.yaml listing deployment.yaml and service.yaml. Which command applies the customized result?",["kubectl apply -f kustomization.yaml","kubectl create -r ./overlay","kubectl apply -k ./overlay","kubectl kustomize apply ./overlay"],2,"-k tells kubectl to build the kustomization in that directory and apply the output. Applying kustomization.yaml with -f would send the Kustomization file itself, which is not a cluster resource.","Kustomize",["With -f, kubectl sends the Kustomization file itself, which is not a resource the API server accepts, instead of building the overlay.","kubectl create -r is not a valid way to build a kustomization; -R is recursive for -f, and no build step happens.",null,"kubectl kustomize only builds and prints YAML; it has no apply subcommand."]],["ck33",0,2,"In a Kustomize overlay you want every resource name to start with prod- and every image nginx to use tag 1.27. Which fields do this?",["commonAnnotations and replicas","nameSuffix and patches","namePrefix and images","generatorOptions and images"],2,"namePrefix prepends to resource names and images overrides image names or tags. nameSuffix appends instead of prepending.","Kustomize",["commonAnnotations adds annotations and replicas changes replica counts; neither renames resources or changes image tags.","nameSuffix appends to names rather than prepending, and patches is a heavier way to change images than the images field.",null,"generatorOptions affects ConfigMap and Secret generators, such as hash suffixes, and does not rename resources."]],["ck34",0,2,"You want to check what an overlay will produce before applying it. Which command prints the rendered YAML?",["kubectl kustomize ./overlay","kubectl diff -f ./overlay","kubectl get -k ./overlay","kubectl explain -k ./overlay"],0,"kubectl kustomize <dir> builds and prints the output without contacting the API for changes. kubectl diff compares against the cluster and needs -k to read a kustomization.","Kustomize",[null,"kubectl diff compares against the live cluster rather than just printing YAML, and with -f it would not build the kustomization.","kubectl get -k reads the named objects back from the cluster; it does not print the locally rendered overlay.","kubectl explain shows field documentation for resource types and does not accept a kustomization directory."]],["ck35",0,2,"After `kubectl apply` you want to wait until a Deployment rollout finishes or fails. Which command?",["kubectl rollout history deployment/web","kubectl get deployment web --watch-only","kubectl wait pod --for=complete","kubectl rollout status deployment/web"],3,"rollout status blocks until the rollout completes or exceeds its progress deadline. rollout history only shows past revisions.","Rolling updates",["rollout history lists past revisions and returns immediately rather than waiting for the current rollout.","--watch-only streams changes to the Deployment object but never exits on success or failure, so it is not a proper wait.","Pods are never complete in a Deployment, and kubectl wait also needs a target; this does not track the rollout.",null]],["ck36",0,2,"You try to change `spec.selector.matchLabels` on an existing apps/v1 Deployment and the API rejects it. Why?",["Selectors can only be changed with kubectl edit, not kubectl apply","The selector of an apps/v1 Deployment is immutable","Labels must be changed on the ReplicaSet first","Selectors cannot contain more than one label"],1,"In apps/v1 the Deployment selector cannot be changed after creation, so you must create a new Deployment. The editing tool makes no difference.","Deployments",["The rule is enforced by the API server, so kubectl edit is rejected in exactly the same way as kubectl apply.",null,"You cannot fix it by editing ReplicaSets; the Deployment controller owns them and the Deployment's selector remains immutable.","Selectors can match on multiple labels; the number of labels has nothing to do with this rejection."]],["ck37",0,3,"An old manifest uses `apiVersion: extensions/v1beta1` for an Ingress and fails on a current cluster. What should it use?",["networking.k8s.io/v1beta1","ingress.k8s.io/v1","apps/v1","networking.k8s.io/v1"],3,"Ingress graduated to networking.k8s.io/v1 and the beta versions were removed in 1.22. networking.k8s.io/v1beta1 is one of those removed versions.","API deprecations",["networking.k8s.io/v1beta1 for Ingress was also removed in 1.22, so the manifest would still fail.","ingress.k8s.io is not a real API group; Ingress lives in networking.k8s.io.","apps/v1 serves Deployments, StatefulSets, DaemonSets and ReplicaSets, not Ingress.",null]],["ck38",0,3,"Which command shows each resource kind with its API group/version and short name on the current cluster?",["kubectl cluster-info","kubectl version","kubectl api-resources","kubectl config view"],2,"api-resources lists every served kind with group/version, short name and whether it is namespaced. kubectl version only reports client and server versions.","API deprecations",["cluster-info prints the control plane and core service addresses, not a list of resource kinds.","kubectl version reports client and server versions only.",null,"config view shows kubeconfig clusters, users and contexts, not the API resources the server offers."]],["ck39",0,3,"A CronJob manifest uses `apiVersion: batch/v1beta1` and is rejected. What should the apiVersion be?",["batch/v2","batch/v1","apps/v1","cronjob/v1"],1,"CronJob is stable in batch/v1, and batch/v1beta1 was removed in Kubernetes 1.25. There is no batch/v2.","API deprecations",["There is no batch/v2 API version; CronJob is served only in batch/v1.",null,"apps/v1 serves workload controllers like Deployments, not CronJobs, which are in the batch group.","cronjob/v1 is not an API group; apiVersion takes the group (batch) and version (v1)."]],["ck40",0,3,"A Pod's readiness probe starts failing while its liveness probe still passes. What happens?",["The Pod is removed from the Service's endpoints","The kubelet restarts the container","The Pod is evicted to another node","The Deployment automatically rolls back to the previous revision"],0,"Readiness failure only stops traffic by removing the Pod from endpoints. Restarting the container is what a liveness failure does.","Probes",[null,"Restarts are triggered by a failing liveness probe; readiness failures never restart the container.","Probes never evict or reschedule Pods; eviction comes from node pressure or preemption.","Deployments do not roll back automatically on probe failures; a rollback requires kubectl rollout undo."]],["ck41",0,3,"A Java app takes up to 3 minutes to start, and its liveness probe kills it during startup. What is the best fix?",["Add a startupProbe that covers the startup time","Remove the liveness probe entirely","Set the liveness probe's timeoutSeconds to 180 seconds","Change restartPolicy to Never"],0,"A startupProbe suspends liveness and readiness checks until it succeeds, protecting slow starts while keeping deadlock detection afterward. A 180-second timeout would make every later check extremely slow.","Probes",[null,"Removing liveness stops the startup kills but also loses automatic recovery from deadlocks after the app is running.","timeoutSeconds is how long one check waits for a response, so 180 seconds would make every later check painfully slow instead of covering startup.","restartPolicy Never is not allowed for Deployment Pods, and it would stop all recovery rather than protecting the startup period."]],["ck42",0,3,"Read this probe: `livenessProbe: {httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080}, periodSeconds: 10, failureThreshold: 3}`. When is the container restarted?",["After one failed check","Only if /healthz returns a 5xx code three times in a minute","After 10 failed checks","After three failed checks in a row, about 30 seconds"],3,"The kubelet probes every 10 seconds and restarts after 3 consecutive failures. Any status outside 200–399, or no response, counts as a failure.","Probes",["failureThreshold 3 means one failure is tolerated; the kubelet waits for three consecutive failures.","Any status outside 200-399 or a timeout counts, not only 5xx, and the failures must be consecutive rather than within a minute.","The threshold is 3 consecutive failures, not 10; 10 is the periodSeconds value.",null]],["ck43",0,3,"A container has no HTTP endpoint but accepts TCP connections on port 5432. Which probe handler is most appropriate?",["httpGet on port 5432","tcpSocket on port 5432","exec running curl localhost:5432","grpc on port 5432"],1,"tcpSocket succeeds if a TCP connection can be opened, which suits a database port. httpGet would fail because the server does not speak HTTP.","Probes",["httpGet sends an HTTP request, which a non-HTTP server like PostgreSQL cannot answer properly, so the probe would fail.",null,"curl speaks HTTP, so against a non-HTTP port it errors, and the image may not even include curl.","A grpc probe needs the app to implement the gRPC health checking protocol, which a plain TCP service does not."]],["ck44",0,3,"Container app in Pod api keeps restarting. Which command shows the output of the previous, crashed instance?",["kubectl describe logs api -c app","kubectl logs api --since=prev","kubectl logs api -c app --previous","kubectl get logs api app -p=false"],2,"--previous (-p) returns logs from the last terminated instance of the container. Current logs may be empty right after a restart.","Container logs",["describe does not have a logs subcommand; it shows object details and events, not container output.","--since takes a duration like 5m, not prev, and it filters current logs rather than returning the crashed instance's output.",null,"There is no kubectl get logs resource, and -p=false would explicitly ask for the current, not previous, container."]],["ck45",0,3,"You want live logs from all Pods labeled app=web, in every container. Which command works?",["kubectl logs deployment -A app=web","kubectl logs -l app=web --all-containers -f","kubectl get logs --selector app=web","kubectl describe pods -l app=web --follow"],1,"-l selects Pods by label, --all-containers includes each container and -f follows output. describe shows events, not log streams.","Container logs",["kubectl logs takes a single resource and a label with -l, not a positional app=web, and -A is not used here.",null,"Logs are not an API resource, so kubectl get logs does not exist.","describe shows Pod details and events, not log streams, and it has no --follow flag."]],["ck46",0,3,"`kubectl top pod` returns \"Metrics API not available\". What is missing?",["The kubelet's --read-only-port","kube-state-metrics in kube-system","A Prometheus Operator CRD","metrics-server running in the cluster"],3,"kubectl top reads the metrics.k8s.io API, which metrics-server provides. kube-state-metrics exposes object state for Prometheus, not the Metrics API.","CLI monitoring",["The read-only port is a legacy kubelet endpoint; enabling it does not provide the metrics.k8s.io API kubectl top needs.","kube-state-metrics exposes object state for Prometheus scraping, not the resource Metrics API that kubectl top reads.","Prometheus Operator CRDs manage Prometheus deployments; they do not serve metrics.k8s.io.",null]],["ck47",0,3,"A Pod shows status ImagePullBackOff. What is the most likely cause?",["Wrong image name or tag, or missing registry credentials","The container exceeded its memory limit","The readiness probe path returns 404","The node has too little free CPU and memory to schedule the Pod"],0,"ImagePullBackOff means the kubelet could not pull the image: wrong name or tag, private registry without imagePullSecrets, or network issues. A memory problem shows as OOMKilled.","Debugging",[null,"Exceeding a memory limit shows as OOMKilled after the container has started, which needs the image to have been pulled.","A failing readiness probe leaves a running container not Ready; it has nothing to do with pulling the image.","Insufficient node capacity leaves the Pod Pending with FailedScheduling, before any image pull is attempted."]],["ck48",0,3,"A container's last state shows `Reason: OOMKilled, Exit Code: 137`. What does this mean?",["The liveness probe failed three times in a row and timed out","The node ran out of disk space","The container exceeded its memory limit and was killed","The image entrypoint was not found"],2,"OOMKilled means memory use passed the limit; 137 is 128 plus SIGKILL (9). A missing entrypoint gives a start error, usually exit code 127 or 128.","Debugging",["A liveness failure restarts the container with a probe-related event, not an OOMKilled reason.","Node disk pressure leads to eviction with a reason like Evicted, not OOMKilled with exit code 137.",null,"A missing entrypoint produces a start error such as exit code 127 or a ContainerCannotRun message, not OOMKilled."]],["ck49",0,3,"A distroless container has no shell, so kubectl exec fails. How can you inspect its processes and network?",["kubectl attach --image=busybox","kubectl cp busybox <pod>:/bin/sh","kubectl debug -it <pod> --image=busybox --target=<container>","kubectl run --overrides with hostPID"],2,"kubectl debug adds an ephemeral container with tools; --target shares the target container's process namespace. Copying a shell binary into a distroless image is unreliable and changes the container.","Debugging",["kubectl attach connects to the existing process's stdio and has no --image flag to bring in tools.","kubectl cp relies on tar inside the container, which a distroless image lacks, and copying binaries in changes the container.",null,"kubectl run creates a separate Pod; even with hostPID it does not join the target Pod's network namespace or container process namespace cleanly."]],["ck50",0,3,"You need to list events in a namespace in the order they happened. Which command?",["kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp","kubectl describe events --order=time","kubectl logs events -n <ns>","kubectl get events -o wide --watch-only=false"],0,"--sort-by with a JSONPath to creationTimestamp orders events by time. describe has no ordering flag, and events are not logs.","CLI monitoring",[null,"kubectl describe has no --order flag, and describing events does not list them sorted by time.","Events are API objects, not container logs, so kubectl logs cannot read them.","-o wide adds columns but does not sort, and --watch-only=false just disables a watch; the output still isn't ordered by time."]],["ck51",0,4,"A vendor installed an Operator, and you need to see which new resource kinds it added. Which command is quickest?",["kubectl get apiservices -o yaml","kubectl get operators --all","kubectl describe nodes","kubectl get crd"],3,"CustomResourceDefinitions register new kinds, so listing CRDs shows what the Operator added. There is no built-in operators resource unless another CRD defines one.","CRDs and Operators",["APIService objects register aggregated API servers, and most Operators add kinds through CRDs, not APIServices.","There is no built-in operators resource; it would only exist if some CRD (such as OLM's) defined it.","describe nodes shows node capacity, conditions and Pods, not which resource kinds exist.",null]],["ck52",0,4,"What best describes an Operator in Kubernetes?",["A cluster admin role with full RBAC rights over every namespace","A controller that manages an app via custom resources","A mutating webhook that injects sidecars","A Helm chart for installing CRDs"],1,"An Operator is a custom controller that watches custom resources and reconciles the application toward the declared state. A Helm chart may install one but is not itself a controller.","CRDs and Operators",["An Operator is software, not an RBAC role; it needs RBAC permissions but is not itself a set of permissions.",null,"A mutating webhook changes objects at admission time; it does not continuously reconcile an application's state.","A Helm chart is a packaging format that may install an Operator, but it is not a controller running in the cluster."]],["ck53",0,4,"In what order does the API server process a request to create a Pod?",["Authorization, authentication, admission","Admission, authentication, authorization","Authentication, admission, authorization","Authentication, authorization, admission"],3,"The server first identifies the caller, then checks permission with RBAC, then runs admission controllers before storing the object. Admission can only act on requests that were already allowed.","AuthN, AuthZ, admission",["The server cannot check permissions before it knows who the caller is, so authentication must come first.","Admission runs last on requests that were already authenticated and authorized, not first.","Authorization must happen before admission; admission controllers only see requests that RBAC already allowed.",null]],["ck54",0,4,"A Pod is rejected with a message from ResourceQuota even though RBAC allows the user to create Pods. Which stage rejected it?",["Authentication","Authorization","Admission control","The scheduler"],2,"ResourceQuota is enforced by an admission controller after authorization succeeds. The scheduler never sees a rejected Pod.","AuthN, AuthZ, admission",["Authentication succeeded because the user was identified; the error comes from a quota check, not identity.","RBAC allowed the create, so authorization passed; ResourceQuota is not an RBAC decision.",null,"The scheduler only sees Pods that were stored; a quota rejection happens before the Pod object exists."]],["ck55",0,4,"You need ServiceAccount app in namespace dev to only get, list and watch Pods in dev. Which object pair fits?",["ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding","Role and RoleBinding in dev","Role in dev and ClusterRoleBinding","ClusterRoleBinding to the view ClusterRole"],1,"A Role and RoleBinding grant namespaced permissions only in dev. A ClusterRoleBinding would grant access in every namespace.","RBAC",["A ClusterRoleBinding grants the permissions in every namespace, which is broader than just dev.",null,"A ClusterRoleBinding cannot reference a Role; it can only bind a ClusterRole, cluster-wide.","Binding the view ClusterRole cluster-wide grants read access to many resources in every namespace, far beyond Pods in dev."]],["ck56",0,4,"Which command checks whether ServiceAccount app in namespace dev may list Secrets there?",["kubectl auth can-i list secrets -n dev --as=system:serviceaccount:dev:app","kubectl auth whoami --sa app -n dev","kubectl get rolebinding app --can-i=secrets","kubectl describe sa app --permissions"],0,"auth can-i with --as impersonates the ServiceAccount using its full username. auth whoami only reports your own identity.","RBAC",[null,"auth whoami only reports your own identity and has no --sa flag to test another subject's permissions.","kubectl get has no --can-i flag; RoleBinding output does not evaluate whether an action is allowed.","describe sa shows the ServiceAccount's tokens and secrets, and there is no --permissions flag."]],["ck57",0,4,"Which command creates a Role named pod-reader with get, list and watch on Pods?",["kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods","kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader --verb=get --resource=pods","kubectl create clusterrole pod-reader --role=get,list,watch","kubectl apply role pod-reader --pods=read"],0,"create role takes --verb and --resource. A RoleBinding links a Role to subjects and does not define verbs itself.","RBAC",[null,"A RoleBinding links subjects to a Role; it does not accept --verb or --resource to define permissions.","--role is not a flag of create clusterrole, and a ClusterRole is cluster-scoped rather than the namespaced Role asked for.","kubectl apply takes manifests with -f, and there is no --pods=read shorthand for permissions."]],["ck58",0,4,"A container has `requests.memory: 256Mi` and `limits.memory: 512Mi`. It grows to 600Mi. What happens?",["The kubelet throttles it back to 512Mi","Nothing, because only requests are enforced","The scheduler moves it to a bigger node","It is OOMKilled and restarted"],3,"Memory cannot be throttled, so exceeding the limit gets the container OOMKilled. Throttling applies to CPU limits.","Requests and limits",["Memory is not compressible, so the kubelet cannot throttle it; only CPU limits are enforced by throttling.","Limits are enforced at runtime by the kernel cgroup; requests only affect scheduling.","The scheduler never moves running Pods; placement happens once, and exceeding a limit leads to a kill, not a move.",null]],["ck59",0,4,"A container has `limits.cpu: 500m` and tries to use two full cores. What happens?",["It is killed with exit code 137","It is throttled to about half a core","The Pod is evicted immediately","It is allowed because CPU is compressible"],1,"500m is 0.5 CPU and CFS quota throttles usage to that. Being killed happens for memory, not CPU.","Requests and limits",["Being killed with 137 happens for exceeding a memory limit; CPU overuse is throttled instead.",null,"Eviction comes from node pressure such as low memory or disk, not from a container hitting its own CPU limit.","CPU being compressible is why it gets throttled rather than killed, but the limit is still enforced, so two cores are not allowed."]],["ck60",0,4,"Every container in a Pod sets CPU and memory requests equal to their limits. Which QoS class does the Pod get?",["BestEffort","Burstable","Guaranteed","Critical"],2,"Requests equal to limits for CPU and memory in all containers gives Guaranteed. Burstable applies when some requests are set but do not match limits.","Requests and limits",["BestEffort is for Pods where no container sets any requests or limits.","Burstable applies when at least one request or limit is set but they do not all match; here they match exactly.",null,"Critical is not a QoS class; the three classes are Guaranteed, Burstable and BestEffort."]],["ck61",0,4,"Namespace team-a has a ResourceQuota on requests.cpu. A Pod without CPU requests is rejected. What prevents this without editing each manifest?",["A PriorityClass with a high value","A LimitRange with default requests","A HorizontalPodAutoscaler","A PodDisruptionBudget"],1,"A LimitRange injects default requests and limits into containers that omit them, satisfying the quota. A PriorityClass affects scheduling order, not quotas.","Quotas",["A PriorityClass affects scheduling order and preemption; it adds no requests, so the quota still rejects the Pod.",null,"An HPA changes replica counts based on metrics and cannot add missing requests to a Pod spec.","A PodDisruptionBudget limits voluntary evictions; it has no effect on quota admission."]],["ck62",0,4,"Which command creates a ConfigMap named app-cfg with key MODE=prod and the contents of app.properties?",["kubectl apply configmap app-cfg MODE=prod app.properties","kubectl create secret generic app-cfg --from-literal=MODE=prod --from-file=app.properties","kubectl create configmap app-cfg --env=MODE=prod --file app.properties","kubectl create configmap app-cfg --from-literal=MODE=prod --from-file=app.properties"],3,"--from-literal adds key=value pairs and --from-file adds a key named after the file. create secret would make a Secret, not a ConfigMap.","ConfigMaps",["kubectl apply needs a manifest via -f and cannot build a ConfigMap from positional key=value and file arguments.","This creates a Secret, not a ConfigMap, even though the flags are right.","create configmap does not have --env or --file flags; the flags are --from-literal, --from-file and --from-env-file.",null]],["ck63",0,4,"A Pod should load every key of ConfigMap app-cfg as an environment variable. Which field does this with the least YAML?",["envFrom with configMapRef: {name: app-cfg}","env with valueFrom.configMapKeyRef for each key","volumes with configMap: {name: app-cfg}","spec.configMapName: app-cfg"],0,"envFrom imports all keys at once. configMapKeyRef works but needs one entry per key, and a volume makes files, not env vars.","ConfigMaps",[null,"configMapKeyRef works but needs a separate env entry for every key, which is more YAML than envFrom.","A configMap volume exposes keys as files in a directory, not as environment variables.","There is no spec.configMapName field in a Pod spec, so the API would reject or ignore it."]],["ck64",0,4,"A ConfigMap is used both as env vars and as a mounted volume (no subPath). You update the ConfigMap. What changes in running Pods?",["Both update within seconds","The env vars update right away; the mounted files stay the same","The mounted files update eventually; env vars do not","Nothing updates until the node reboots"],2,"The kubelet refreshes projected ConfigMap volumes, but env vars are read only at container start. Env vars change only after the Pod restarts.","ConfigMaps",["Environment variables are read only when the container starts, so they do not change in a running Pod.","It is the reverse: env vars stay fixed until restart, while mounted files are refreshed by the kubelet.",null,"A node reboot is not needed; mounted files refresh after the kubelet sync period, and env vars update after a Pod restart."]],["ck65",0,4,"A teammate says Kubernetes Secrets are secure because their values look scrambled in YAML. What is accurate?",["Values are AES-encrypted by kubectl on your machine before they are uploaded","Values are hashed and cannot be read back","Values are only base64-encoded unless encryption at rest is set up","Values are stored only in node memory"],2,"base64 is an encoding that anyone can reverse; protection comes from RBAC and API server encryption at rest. Secrets can be read back, so they are not hashed.","Secrets",["kubectl does not encrypt Secret values client-side; the data field is just base64 text.","Secret values must be readable by the Pods that use them, so they are encoded, not hashed.",null,"Secrets are stored in etcd like other objects; they are only kept in tmpfs on nodes when mounted into Pods."]],["ck66",0,4,"Which command creates a Secret named db-cred with password=S3cret?",["kubectl create secret generic db-cred --from-literal=password=S3cret","kubectl create secret tls db-cred --from-literal=password=S3cret","kubectl create configmap db-cred --secret password=S3cret","kubectl create secret db-cred password=S3cret --type=opaque"],0,"generic is the subcommand for Opaque Secrets from literals or files. The tls type needs --cert and --key files.","Secrets",[null,"The tls type requires --cert and --key files and does not accept --from-literal.","This would create a ConfigMap, and create configmap has no --secret flag.","create secret needs a subtype like generic, and values are passed with --from-literal, not as positional arguments."]],["ck67",0,4,"Read this snippet: `env: [{name: DB_PASS, valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: db-cred, key: pwd}}}]`. The Secret has only a key named password. What happens?",["DB_PASS is set to an empty string","The Pod starts and ignores DB_PASS","Kubernetes falls back to the first key found in the Secret","The container fails to start with a config error"],3,"A secretKeyRef to a missing key causes CreateContainerConfigError unless optional: true is set. Kubernetes never guesses another key.","Secrets",["Kubernetes never substitutes an empty string for a missing key unless optional: true is set, in which case the variable is simply not set.","Without optional: true the missing key is an error; the container does not start while ignoring the variable.","Kubernetes never guesses another key; references must name an existing key exactly.",null]],["ck68",0,4,"Pod web must run as ServiceAccount web-sa, which already exists. Which Pod field sets this?",["metadata.serviceAccount: web-sa","spec.serviceAccountName: web-sa","spec.containers[].serviceAccount: web-sa","spec.securityContext.serviceAccount: web-sa"],1,"spec.serviceAccountName selects the ServiceAccount for the whole Pod. It is not a per-container or securityContext setting.","ServiceAccounts",["The ServiceAccount is set in spec, not metadata, so metadata.serviceAccount has no effect.",null,"ServiceAccounts are chosen per Pod, not per container, so there is no container-level serviceAccount field.","securityContext controls UIDs, capabilities and similar settings; it has no serviceAccount field."]],["ck69",0,4,"An app never calls the Kubernetes API. How do you stop an API token from being mounted into its Pods?",["Delete the default ServiceAccount","Remove the Pod's RoleBinding","Set readOnlyRootFilesystem: true","Set automountServiceAccountToken: false"],3,"automountServiceAccountToken: false on the Pod or ServiceAccount skips mounting the token. Deleting the default ServiceAccount breaks Pod creation in the namespace.","ServiceAccounts",["Deleting the default ServiceAccount breaks Pod creation in the namespace and it is recreated automatically anyway.","RoleBindings control what the token may do; removing one still mounts the token into the Pod.","readOnlyRootFilesystem blocks writes to the container filesystem but does not stop the token volume being mounted.",null]],["ck70",0,4,"A container must run as UID 1000 and never as root. Which securityContext settings fit?",["privileged: false and runAsGroup: 0","fsGroup: 1000 and allowPrivilegeEscalation: true","runAsUser: 1000 and runAsNonRoot: true","runAsUser: 0 and readOnlyRootFilesystem: true"],2,"runAsUser sets the UID and runAsNonRoot makes the kubelet refuse to start the container as UID 0. privileged: false alone does not stop the process running as root.","Security contexts",["privileged: false does not prevent running as root, and runAsGroup: 0 sets the primary group to root.","fsGroup sets volume group ownership, not the process UID, and allowing privilege escalation weakens security.",null,"runAsUser: 0 is root, which is exactly what must be avoided; a read-only filesystem does not change the UID."]],["ck71",0,4,"A network tool in a container needs to change interface settings but nothing else privileged. What is the least-privilege approach?",["Set privileged: true","Drop ALL and add the NET_ADMIN capability","Run the container as root with hostNetwork","Set allowPrivilegeEscalation: true"],1,"Adding only NET_ADMIN after dropping all capabilities grants just what the tool needs. privileged: true gives nearly full host access.","Security contexts",["privileged: true grants nearly all host capabilities and device access, far more than changing interfaces needs.",null,"Running as root with hostNetwork gives access to the node's network stack, which is broader than the container needs.","allowPrivilegeEscalation: true only permits setuid-style escalation; it does not grant NET_ADMIN and weakens security."]],["ck72",0,4,"Namespace apps is labeled `pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted`. A Pod without runAsNonRoot and without dropping capabilities is submitted. What happens?",["Pod Security Admission rejects it","It runs and a warning is logged","It is mutated to comply and runs","It is scheduled only on tainted nodes"],0,"In enforce mode, Pods that violate the restricted standard are rejected at admission. Pod Security Admission validates only; it does not mutate Pods.","Admission control",[null,"Logging a warning is the warn mode behavior; enforce mode rejects the Pod.","Pod Security Admission only validates; it never mutates Pods to make them comply.","Pod Security Admission does not influence scheduling or taints; it acts at admission time before the Pod is stored."]],["ck73",0,5,"Which command creates a ClusterIP Service for Deployment web that listens on port 80 and forwards to container port 8080?",["kubectl expose deployment web --port=80 --target-port=8080","kubectl create service web --port=8080:80","kubectl expose pod web --node-port=80","kubectl port-forward deployment/web 80:8080"],0,"expose copies the Deployment's selector and sets port and targetPort. port-forward only tunnels from your machine and creates no Service.","Services",[null,"create service needs a type subcommand like clusterip, ports are given with --tcp=80:8080, and it would not copy the Deployment's selector.","This exposes a Pod named web rather than the Deployment, and --node-port is not the flag for a ClusterIP Service.","port-forward opens a temporary tunnel from your machine and creates no Service object."]],["ck74",0,5,"A Service has no endpoints even though its Pods are Running and Ready. What is the most likely cause?",["The Service type is ClusterIP","The namespace has no NetworkPolicy allowing traffic to the Pods","The Pods have no resource limits","The Service selector does not match the Pod labels"],3,"Endpoints are built from Pods that match the selector and are ready. ClusterIP is the normal type and does not block endpoints.","Services",["ClusterIP is the default and normal Service type; it does not prevent endpoints from being created.","NetworkPolicies filter traffic but do not affect which Pods appear in a Service's endpoints.","Resource limits are unrelated to endpoint selection, which is based on labels and readiness.",null]],["ck75",0,5,"Pods in namespace shop must call Service api in namespace backend. Which DNS name always works?",["api.shop.svc.cluster.local","api.backend.svc.cluster.local","backend.api.cluster.local","api.svc.backend.local"],1,"The pattern is <service>.<namespace>.svc.<cluster-domain>. The short name api would only resolve inside backend itself.","Services",["This name points at a Service called api in namespace shop, not the one in backend.",null,"The order is wrong and svc is missing; the pattern is service, then namespace, then svc and the cluster domain.","The namespace and svc parts are swapped and the cluster domain is wrong, so this name does not resolve."]],["ck76",0,5,"Read this Service: `type: NodePort`, `port: 80`, `targetPort: 8080`, `nodePort: 30080`. How does an external client reach the app?",["http://<node-ip>:80","http://<cluster-ip>:30080","http://<node-ip>:30080","http://<pod-ip>:30080"],2,"NodePort opens 30080 on every node and forwards to port 80 of the Service, then 8080 on the Pods. The ClusterIP is only reachable inside the cluster.","Services",["Port 80 is the Service port on the ClusterIP; it is not opened on the node's IP.","30080 is opened on node IPs, and the ClusterIP is only reachable from inside the cluster anyway.",null,"Pods listen on the targetPort 8080; nothing listens on 30080 at the Pod IP, and Pod IPs are usually not reachable from outside."]],["ck77",0,5,"A StatefulSet needs DNS records for each Pod (db-0.db, db-1.db) rather than one virtual IP. Which Service setting provides this?",["type: ExternalName","clusterIP: None (headless)","sessionAffinity: ClientIP","externalTrafficPolicy: Local"],1,"A headless Service has no virtual IP and returns per-Pod DNS records for StatefulSet members. sessionAffinity only pins clients to Pods behind a normal ClusterIP.","Services",["ExternalName returns a CNAME to an outside host and has no connection to the StatefulSet's Pods.",null,"sessionAffinity pins a client to one Pod behind a normal virtual IP; it creates no per-Pod DNS records.","externalTrafficPolicy: Local controls node-local routing for external traffic, not per-Pod DNS names."]],["ck78",0,5,"Apps should call an external database as `db` inside the cluster, which maps to db.example.com. Which Service type fits?",["ClusterIP with no selector","LoadBalancer","NodePort","ExternalName"],3,"ExternalName returns a DNS CNAME to the external host. A selector-less ClusterIP needs manual endpoints with IP addresses instead.","Services",["A ClusterIP without a selector needs manual endpoints with IP addresses; it cannot map to a DNS hostname.","A LoadBalancer exposes in-cluster Pods to outside clients, the opposite direction of this requirement.","NodePort exposes in-cluster Pods on node ports; it does not alias an external hostname.",null]],["ck79",0,5,"Read this NetworkPolicy: `podSelector: {}`, `policyTypes: [Ingress]`, and no ingress rules. What does it do?",["Denies all ingress to every Pod in the namespace","Allows all ingress to every Pod in the namespace","Denies all egress from the namespace","Has no effect because no rules are listed"],0,"An empty podSelector selects all Pods; with Ingress in policyTypes and no rules, no incoming traffic is allowed. Egress is untouched because it is not listed.","NetworkPolicy",[null,"With Ingress in policyTypes and an empty ingress list, nothing is allowed in; allow-all needs a rule with an empty entry.","Egress is not in policyTypes, so outgoing traffic is not restricted by this policy.","An empty rule list is meaningful: it selects all Pods and allows no ingress, which is the default-deny pattern."]],["ck80",0,5,"You apply a valid NetworkPolicy, but traffic it should block still flows. The cluster uses a CNI with no policy support. Why?",["NetworkPolicies need a restart of kube-proxy","NetworkPolicies apply only to NodePort Services","The CNI plugin must enforce NetworkPolicies","Policies take effect only for new namespaces"],2,"The API stores NetworkPolicies, but the network plugin (such as Calico or Cilium) enforces them. kube-proxy handles Service routing, not policy.","NetworkPolicy",["kube-proxy implements Service routing, not NetworkPolicy, so restarting it changes nothing.","NetworkPolicies apply to Pod traffic in general, regardless of Service type.",null,"Policies apply to matching Pods in existing namespaces as soon as an enforcing CNI sees them."]],["ck81",0,5,"A NetworkPolicy on backend Pods has one `from` element containing both `namespaceSelector: {matchLabels: {team: web}}` and `podSelector: {matchLabels: {role: fe}}`. Who is allowed?",["Any Pod in team=web namespaces, plus any role=fe Pod","Only role=fe Pods in the backend namespace","Only role=fe Pods in namespaces labeled team=web","All Pods in every namespace"],2,"Selectors in the same from element are ANDed. Listing them as two separate elements would OR them and allow both groups.","NetworkPolicy",["That would be the OR behavior of two separate from elements; in one element the selectors are ANDed.","The namespaceSelector widens the scope to team=web namespaces instead of limiting it to the backend namespace.",null,"The selectors restrict traffic to a specific set of Pods; nothing here allows every namespace."]],["ck82",0,5,"After adding an egress default-deny policy, Pods cannot resolve any Service names. What must you allow?",["Egress to kube-dns on UDP and TCP port 53","Egress to the API server on port 6443","Ingress from kube-proxy on port 10256","Egress to the node's kubelet on port 10250"],0,"DNS lookups go to the cluster DNS Pods on port 53, which the default-deny egress blocks. The API server is not involved in name resolution.","NetworkPolicy",[null,"Name resolution does not go through the API server, so allowing 6443 does not fix DNS lookups.","The problem is outgoing DNS traffic, not incoming traffic, and kube-proxy's health port is unrelated to DNS.","The kubelet port is used for logs and exec from the API server; Pods do not resolve names through it."]],["ck83",0,5,"Only Pods labeled app=frontend may reach Pods labeled app=api on TCP 8080. Which ingress rule belongs in a policy selecting app=api?",["from: ipBlock 0.0.0.0/0; ports: TCP 8080","to: podSelector app=frontend; ports: TCP 8080","from: namespaceSelector app=frontend; ports: 8080","from: podSelector app=frontend; ports: TCP 8080"],3,"Ingress rules use from peers, and a podSelector matches Pods in the policy's namespace. namespaceSelector would match namespace labels, not Pod labels.","NetworkPolicy",["ipBlock 0.0.0.0/0 allows traffic from any IP, not just frontend Pods.","to peers are used in egress rules; an ingress rule needs from.","namespaceSelector matches labels on namespaces, not on Pods, so it would not select app=frontend Pods.",null]],["ck84",0,5,"An Ingress is created, but `kubectl get ingress` shows no address and requests never reach the app. What is most likely missing?",["A NodePort Service in kube-system","An Ingress controller for its ingressClassName","A LoadBalancer Service type on the backend application","A NetworkPolicy allowing port 443"],1,"The Ingress resource is only configuration; a controller such as ingress-nginx must implement it. Backends can be ordinary ClusterIP Services.","Ingress",["NodePort Services in kube-system are not required; without a controller nothing implements the Ingress.",null,"Backends can be ordinary ClusterIP Services; LoadBalancer is not needed behind an Ingress.","A NetworkPolicy would not give the Ingress an address; the missing address means no controller has picked it up."]],["ck85",0,5,"An Ingress path is `/api` with `pathType: Prefix`. Which request matches it?",["/apis","/API","/v1/api","/api/v1/users"],3,"Prefix matches whole path segments, so /api/v1/users matches but /apis does not. Matching is case-sensitive, so /API fails.","Ingress",["Prefix matching works on whole path segments, so /apis is a different segment and does not match /api.","Path matching is case-sensitive, so /API does not match /api.","The prefix must match from the start of the path; /v1/api starts with /v1.",null]],["ck86",0,5,"Which command creates an Ingress named web that routes host shop.example.com, all paths, to Service web on port 80?",["kubectl create service ingress web --host=shop.example.com","kubectl expose ingress web --host=shop.example.com --port=80","kubectl create ingress web --rule=\"shop.example.com/*=web:80\"","kubectl apply ingress web shop.example.com=web:80"],2,"create ingress uses --rule=host/path=service:port, and /* makes a Prefix path. expose makes Services, not Ingresses.","Ingress",["create service has no ingress subtype, and Services cannot do host-based HTTP routing.","kubectl expose creates Services, not Ingress objects.",null,"kubectl apply needs a manifest file and does not accept a rule as positional arguments."]],["ck87",0,5,"An Ingress must serve HTTPS for shop.example.com. What does its tls section reference?",["A ConfigMap containing the certificate","A Secret of type kubernetes.io/tls","A ServiceAccount token","A PersistentVolumeClaim with the key files"],1,"The tls section names the host and a Secret holding tls.crt and tls.key, created with kubectl create secret tls. ConfigMaps are not used for keys.","Ingress",["The Ingress tls section references Secrets only; ConfigMaps are not used for certificates or keys.",null,"ServiceAccount tokens authenticate to the Kubernetes API and have nothing to do with serving HTTPS.","Ingress controllers do not read certificates from volumes; the tls section names a Secret."]],["ck88",0,5,"You need to test Service db on port 5432 from your laptop without exposing it outside the cluster. Which command?",["kubectl port-forward svc/db 5432:5432","kubectl expose svc db --type=NodePort","kubectl proxy --port=5432","kubectl run tunnel --port=5432"],0,"port-forward tunnels a local port to the Service through the API server without changing the cluster. Switching to NodePort would expose it on every node.","Services",[null,"Switching to NodePort exposes the database on every node, which is exactly what should be avoided.","kubectl proxy exposes the Kubernetes API on a local port, not a raw TCP connection to the db Service.","kubectl run creates a new Pod and does not create a tunnel to your laptop."]],["ck89",0,5,"A Service defines `port: 80` and `targetPort: http`. What does targetPort: http refer to?",["A named containerPort called http in the Pod spec","The HTTP protocol, which forces Layer 7 routing","Port 80 on the node","An Ingress rule named http"],0,"targetPort can reference a containerPort by name, so Pods can use different port numbers behind the same name. Services do not do Layer 7 routing.","Services",[null,"Services work at Layer 4; a port name like http does not force any protocol-aware routing.","Node ports are set by nodePort; targetPort refers to the Pod side, not the node.","Services do not reference Ingress rules; targetPort names a container port."]],["ck90",0,5,"curl to a Service times out. The endpoints list correct Pod IPs on port 80, but the containers listen on 8080. What is wrong?",["The Service selector is wrong","CoreDNS is not running","The Pods are failing their readiness probes and are not Ready","The Service targetPort does not match the container port"],3,"Endpoints carry the targetPort, so traffic goes to 80 where nothing listens. A wrong selector would give an empty endpoint list instead.","Services",["A wrong selector would leave endpoints empty or pointing to other Pods; here the endpoints are the correct Pods.","DNS is working, since the Service resolves and has endpoints; the timeout is because of the wrong port.","Pods failing readiness would not be listed as ready endpoints, but here the correct Pods are listed.",null]]]);
